# Analyst Notes

Windows services are one of the most commonly abused persistence mechanisms. Adversaries often create new services or modify existing ones to execute malware with elevated privileges and survive system reboots.

Although service management is a routine administrative activity, defenders should pay close attention to service creation, changes to service binaries, or services pointing to executables outside standard Windows directories. Combining this detection with registry, PowerShell, and file creation telemetry provides a much stronger view of potential attacker persistence.

